    "summary": "Epic Truckin'>Drums'>The Other One jam and fiery Good Lovin'",
    "blurb": "The show shines with a marathon Truckin' → Drums → The Other One sequence that rolls into a punchy Casey Jones. A standout moment is the scorching Good Lovin' captured on an upgraded tape. Acoustic opener and solid third‑set numbers round out a classic late‑1970 performance.",
    "review": "Port Chester’s Capitol Theater on November 7, 1970 delivered one of the band’s most memorable improvisational stretches. After a warm acoustic set that featured Ripple and Uncle John’s Band, the electric second set built momentum into an almost legendary Truckin' → Drums → The Other One suite lasting over sixteen minutes before bursting back into a driving Casey Jones. That jam is the centerpiece of the night and is captured best on the Warner audience tape despite its uneven levels – listeners who apply a little EQ are rewarded with a vivid glimpse of the band’s exploratory fire.\n\nThe third set kept the energy high, highlighted by a scorching Good Lovin' that has been restored to crystal‑clear quality in a recent upgrade. While several audience recordings suffer from cuts and fluctuating volume, the Warner tape remains the most complete source for the show’s key moments, making it the go‑to version for fans who want to experience this era of raw, unfiltered Dead magic.",
